      "generated_answer": "In jurisdictions where insanity is an affirmative defense, the defendant typically has the burden of production (to introduce evidence of insanity) and sometimes the burden of persuasion (to prove insanity by a preponderance of the evidence or clear and convincing evidence). The question specifies it is an affirmative defense, which generally means the defendant must both present evidence (burden of production) and persuade the jury (burden of persuasion), though not beyond a reasonable doubt. Therefore, the most accurate answer is that the husband has the burden of production and potentially the burden of persuasion, but among the choices, the one always required is the burden of production.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
