      "generated_answer": "We need $ n+2 \\mid 3(n+3)(n^2+9) $. Let $ d = n+2 $, so $ n = d-2 $. Substitute:\n\n$$\n3(n+3)(n^2+9) = 3(d+1)((d-2)^2 + 9) = 3(d+1)(d^2 - 4d + 13).\n$$\n\nWe require $ d \\mid 3(d+1)(d^2 - 4d + 13) $. Since $ \\gcd(d, d+1) = 1 $, and $ \\gcd(d, d^2 - 4d + 13) = \\gcd(d, 13) $, it follows $ d \\mid 3 \\cdot 13 = 39 $. So $ d $ is a positive divisor of 39: $ d \\in \\{1,3,13,39\\} $. Then $ n = d - 2 $ gives $ n = -1, 1, 11, 37 $. Only positive integers are $ n = 1, 11, 37 $, and their sum is $ 1 + 11 + 37 = 49 $.\n\n$$\n\\boxed{49}\n$$",
