    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **E**, as the stomach is protected by a thick mucus layer that acts as a physical barrier, low permeability of the epithelial cells to ions (especially H⁺), and rapid renewal of the surface epithelium—collectively preventing self-digestion by HCl and pepsin.\n\nOption C is incorrect because the stomach lining secretes *acidic* (not alkaline) mucus; while bicarbonate is secreted *with* mucus to buffer acid near the epithelium, the primary protection is the mucus-bicarbonate barrier, not a general alkaline substance.\n\nOptions A, B, F, I, and J are biologically inaccurate; G is partially true (lining regenerates every 3–6 days, not 10–15), but incomplete without the other protective mechanisms emphasized in E.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
