      "generated_answer": "We analyze the equation $x^{\\log y} = y^{\\log x}$. Taking natural logs of both sides gives $\\log y \\cdot \\ln x = \\log x \\cdot \\ln y$. Using change-of-base ($\\log = \\log_{10}$), we have $\\frac{\\ln y}{\\ln 10} \\cdot \\ln x = \\frac{\\ln x}{\\ln 10} \\cdot \\ln y$, which simplifies to an identity: both sides equal $\\frac{\\ln x \\ln y}{\\ln 10}$. Thus, the equation holds for all $x > 0$, $y > 0$ (where logs are defined), i.e., the entire open first quadrant.\n\n$\\boxed{G}$",
