    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7462",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Can we use the method of compass and straightedge construction to construct the edge length of a cube, such that the volume of the cube is equal to X time the volume of a given cube, where X belongs to the set {{3,17,8,27,343,1331}}? Return the answer list for the respective values of X with 1 for yes and 0 for no.\n\nOptions: \nA. [1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0]\nB. [1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0]\nC. [0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 1]\nD. [0, 1, 1, 1, 0, 0]\nE. [0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 1]\nF. [0, 1, 0, 1, 0, 1]\nG. [0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0]\nH. [1, 1, 1, 0, 0, 1]\nI. [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]\nJ. [1, 0, 0, 1, 1, 0]\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We are asked whether the edge length of a cube with volume $ X \\cdot V $ (where $ V $ is the volume of a given cube) can be constructed with compass and straightedge. This requires constructing $ \\sqrt[3]{X} $ times the original edge length. Compass-and-straightedge constructions can only produce lengths that are constructible numbers — those obtainable via nested square roots, not cube roots. Thus, $ \\sqrt[3]{X} $ must be rational (i.e., $ X $ must be a perfect cube). From the set $ \\{3, 17, 8, 27, 343, 1331\\} $, the perfect cubes are $ 8 = 2^3 $, $ 27 = 3^3 $, $ 343 = 7^3 $, and $ 1331 = 11^3 $; the others ($ 3, 17 $) are not. So answer is [0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 1], which corresponds to option C.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
