es a hexagonal cross-section: the triangular ends combine into a hexagon. So the solid is a hexagonal prism, which has $2$ hexagonal bases and $6$ rectangular lateral faces → **8 faces**. But 8 isn't an option.\n\nRe-examining: Attaching three prisms *by rectangular faces* means aligning them so rectangular faces coincide. If placed in a triangular ring? No—likely in a straight line.\n\nCorrect counting: Each prism contributes 2 triangles (total 6), but only the two end triangles remain visible. The interior triangles are glued? No—triangles are not used for gluing; only rectangular faces are attached. So triangles stay exposed on all three prisms: 6 triangular faces. Rectangular faces: each prism has 3 rectangles; when attaching, two rectangles per adjacency are glued (2 adjacencies → 4 rectangles glued away), so remaining rectangles: $9 - 4 = 5$. Total faces: $6 + 5 = 11$? Still not matching.\n\nWait—the key is \"coplanar faces\": when prisms are aligned side-by-side, the rectangular faces on the same side become coplanar and merge. For three prisms in a row: top rectangles of all three are coplanar → 1 face; same for bottom → 1 face; front and back rectangles (assuming aligning along the \"length\")—if aligned along one rectangular face, then the other two rectangular faces remain separate per prism: so 2 sides × 3 = 6, and the glued faces are gone. Plus 6 triangles? That's too many.\n\nActually, standard interpretation: align three prisms end-to-end along their rectangular faces such that the rectangular faces coincide perfectly, forming a longer solid. The result is a *pentagonal* cross-section? No.\n\nLooking up known problem: 3 triangular prisms attached rectangular-face-to-rectangular-face in a row form a solid with 10 faces: 2 triangular ends, 6 rectangular sides (3 top, 3 bottom—each set coplanar → 2 faces? No).\n\nWait: Final correct count: Each attachment reduces total faces by 2 (since two faces become one, net -1 per attachment, but if three prisms are