    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: a 12 meter long wire is cut into two pieces . if the longer piece is then used to form a perimeter of a square , what is the probability that the area of the square will be more than 4 if the original wire was cut at an arbitrary point ?\n\nOptions: \nA. 1 / 6\nB. 2 / 3\nC. 3 / 10\nD. 1 / 3\nE. 2 / 5\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let the cut be at a point $ x $ meters from one end, where $ 0 < x < 12 $. Without loss of generality, assume $ x \\leq 6 $ (since the longer piece is always used), so the longer piece is $ 12 - x $, and the side of the square is $ \\frac{12 - x}{4} $. The area is $ \\left(\\frac{12 - x}{4}\\right)^2 = \\frac{(12 - x)^2}{16} $. We want this area $ > 4 $, so:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(12 - x)^2}{16} > 4 \\Rightarrow (12 - x)^2 > 64 \\Rightarrow 12 - x > 8 \\Rightarrow x < 4\n$$\n\nSince $ x $ must be less than 6 for the other piece to be longer, valid cut points are $ x < 4 $. So the favorable interval is $ 0 < x < 4 $, over the possible $ 0 < x < 6 $ (due to symmetry), giving probability $ \\frac{4}{6} = \\frac{2}{3} $.\n\nHowever, note: the cut is arbitrary along the full wire, so $ x $ ranges from 0 to 12. The longer piece is used, so effective cut points for the longer piece being used form are symmetric. The longer piece is $ \\max(x, 12 - x) $, which must be $ > 6 $. For the resulting square to have area $ > 4 $, the side $ > 2 $, so perimeter $ > 8 $. So the longer piece must be $ > 8 $, i.e., $ \\max(x, 12 - x) > 8 $. This happens when $ x < 4 $ or $ x > 8 $. So favorable length is $ 4 $ to $ 8 $ excluded? Wait: $ x < 4 $ or $ x > 8 $. Interval length: $ 4 + 4 = 8 $. Total: 12. Probability = $ \\frac{8}{12} = \\frac{2}{3} $.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
      "success": true,
