      "generated_answer": "We are given the frequency-domain system function $ H(j\\omega) = \\frac{j2\\omega}{1 + 2j\\omega} $, and the input $ V(t) = \\cos t $, whose Fourier transform is $ \\pi[\\delta(\\omega + 1) + \\delta(\\omega - 1)] $.  \n\nThe output in the frequency domain is $ Y(j\\omega) = H(j\\omega) \\cdot \\mathcal{F}\\{\\cos t\\} $, so we evaluate $ H(j\\omega) $ at $ \\omega = \\pm 1 $. At $ \\omega = 1 $, $ H(j1) = \\frac{j2}{1 + 2j} = \\frac{2j(1 - 2j)}{(1 + 2j)(1 - 2j)} = \\frac{2j + 4}{5} = \\frac{4}{5} + j\\frac{2}{5} $, and at $ \\omega = -1 $, $ H(-j1) = \\frac{-j2}{1 - 2j} = \\frac{-2j(1 + 2j)}{(1 - 2j)(1 + 2j)} = \\frac{-2j + 4}{5} = \\frac{4}{5} - j\\frac{2}{5} $.\n\nUsing Euler’s formula, the time-domain response is the inverse transform of $ Y(j\\omega) $, resulting in $ \\frac{4}{5}\\cos t - \\frac{2}{5}\\sin t $.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
