      "generated_answer": "The patient presents with acute limb ischemia 2 days post–femoral-popliteal bypass: new severe foot pain, pallor, coolness, and pulselessness despite a strong femoral pulse and weak popliteal pulse, indicating graft occlusion or thrombosis. In this setting, immediate surgical exploration (Option C) is the most appropriate management to evaluate and salvage the limb, as time is critical for preserving viability. Non-invasive imaging (e.g., Doppler US, CT, MRI) or medical therapy (e.g., heparin, tPA) may be considered in subacute cases but are not first-line for a pulseless, painful, pale limb—surgical intervention is standard.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
