ays **d4c5** — but where is the bishop on d4? Earlier: **e3d4** was a move: “e3d4” — yes! Move 40: “e3d4” — so White bishop moved from e3 to d4. Then it stayed there until **d4c5**.\n\nSo bishop on d4 → c5, capturing knight.\n\nBlack to move: rook on d6 is attacked by bishop on c5 and rook on b7.\n\nBest move: **d6e6** — rook moves to e6, away from the attack. Also, **d6c6** is possible, but **e6** keeps the rook on the 6th rank, which is strong.\n\nBut wait — after **d4c5**, White has rook on b7 and bishop on c5. Black’s rook on **d6** is under double attack.\n\nHowever, look at **e6** — is it attacked? White has bishop on c5: diagonal to e6? c5-e7, not e6. Bishop on c5 attacks d6, e7, f8 — not e6. And rook on b7 attacks d7, not e6? b7 attacks the b-file, and squares a8, c7, d6, e5, f4, etc. So yes, **b7 attacks d6**.\n\nRook on b7 moves along the b-file, not c-file. So b7 attacks d6? No! b7 to d6 is two files right, one rank down: not a straight line. Rook on b7 can attack: a7, c7, d7, e7, f7, g7, h7 — vertical. And b6, b5, b4, b3, b2, b1 — horizontal. **Does b7 attack d6?** No. Only if on same rank or file. So bishop on c5 attacks d6 (diagonal), but rook on b7 does not attack d6.\n\nSo only one attacker: **bishop on c5 attacks d6**.\n\nSo Black needs to move rook from d6 — possible moves: d5, c6, e6, d7.\n\nd7? Might be met by Bxd7? But knight is gone.\n\nMost natural: **d6e6** — safe, keeps rook centralized.\n\nBut look ahead: White has bishop on c5, rook on b7, queen is gone, king on g1. Black has bishop on d1, rook on e6? Not yet—current rook is on d6.\n\nAnother idea: **d6c6** — but c6 may be vulnerable after White’s next move b7b5 or something? Not urgent.\n\nBut let’s see the actual winning idea: White has bishop on c5 and rook on b7. Black has bishop on **d1** (from f3d1), and rook on **e6**? No, rook was on e1 → e6 → d6? The sequence: earlier: **e8e1** → **d1g4** → **e1e8** → **e8e6** → then **e6d6**. So rook moved from e6 to d6. So e6 is now empty.\n\nSo