vessel, stating that the physical body is the vessel that holds both the mind (mens) and spirit (anima) of a human being. Neither the mind nor spirit can survive independent of the body. Thus Lucretius states that once the vessel (the body) shatters (dies) its contents (mind and spirit) can no longer exist. So, as a simple ceasing-to-be, death can be neither good nor bad for this being. Being completely devoid of sensation and thought, a dead person cannot miss being alive. According to Lucretius, fear of death is a projection of terrors experienced in life, of pain that only a living (intact) mind can feel. Lucretius also puts forward the 'symmetry argument' against the fear of death. In it, he says that people who fear the prospect of eternal non-existence after death should think back to the eternity of non-existence before their birth, which probably did not cause them much suffering.\n\nQuestion: Whose atomism does Epicurus call upon?\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Epicurus calls upon the atomism of Democritus to support his claim that the universe operates through natural, non-divine mechanisms.  \nThis atomic theory, which posits that all matter is composed of eternal, indivisible particles moving in void space, helps refute fears of divine intervention or punishment.  \nLucretius later expanded these ideas to argue for a purely materialist explanation of nature and the absence of an afterlife.\n\n\\boxed{\\text{Democritus}}",
