  {
    "global index": "NarrativeQA_927",
    "prompt": "Please read the following context and answer the question based on its content.\n\nContext:  Benjamin Bathurst, a British diplomat, disappears whilst staying at an inn in Prussia. Piper describes Bathurst in the story as \"a rather stout gentleman, of past middle age.\" However, the real Bathurst was born in 1784, and thus 25 years old at the time of his disappearance.\nThis story posits that Bathurst slipped into a parallel universe. This event was referenced in the Paratime story \"Police Operation\", also written by Piper. The point of divergence from our history is the Battle of Quebec on December 31, 1775 in which Benedict Arnold is killed instead of merely wounded, leading to the victory of British General John Burgoyne over his American counterpart Horatio Gates at the Battles of Saratoga on September 19 and October 7, 1777. Consequently, the American Revolution and the French Revolution were both failures and there were no Napoleonic Wars.\nThe alternate Bathurst served as the lieutenant governor of the Crown Colony of Georgia. Napoleon Bonaparte is a colonel in the French Army who is considered a brilliant tactician. Charles Maurice de Talleyrand-PĂŠrigord has remained in ecclesiastical orders and risen to become a Catholic Cardinal as well as Louis XVI's Chief Minister. George Washington was killed in battle at Doylestown, Pennsylvania during the short-lived rebellion of the colonies in British North America. Moreover, Thomas Jefferson - the author of the American rebels' Declaration of Philadelphia - fled to Havana and eventually died in the Principality of Liechtenstein several years prior to 1809, while James Madison is in exile in Switzerland. The Bathurst from our timeline is judged to be either insane, or a spy, and imprisoned. He attempts to escape and is fatally shot. There was also a theory that he was his counterpart's half-brother. However, it is noted that there is no evidence to support this.\nBathurst's diplomatic documents are read by a high ranking British officer. He is amused by Talleyrand's role as Bonaparte's advisor and ĂŠminence grise, a role that he finds plausible. However, he is especially puzzled by references to a British general named \"Wellington.\" In the final line of the story, the British officer is revealed to be Sir Arthur Wellesley - known in our reality as the Duke of Wellington. He attained the title by way of his victories in the Napoleonic Wars, which never took place in this universe.\n\nQuestion: How did Benedict Arnold's death change history?\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Benedict Arnold's death at the Battle of Quebec in 1775 led to British General John Burgoyne defeating Horatio Gates at the Battles of Saratoga in 1777. This turned the tide against the American Revolution, causing it to fail and preventing the chain of events that led to the French Revolution and the Napoleonic Wars. As a result, Europe and the Americas developed under continued British and monarchical dominance, drastically altering historical figures' fates and roles. \n\n\\boxed{{Benedict Arnold's death led to a British victory in the American Revolution, resulting in the suppression of both the American and French Revolutions and preventing the rise of Napoleon and the Napoleonic Wars.}}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 575,
        "output_tokens": 149,
        "total_tokens": 724
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": 5.5725e-05,
    "accuracy": 0.2606382978723404,
    "for_optimality": true
  },
  {
    "global index": "NarrativeQA_927",
    "prompt": "Please read the following context and answer the question based on its content.\n\nContext:  Benjamin Bathurst, a British diplomat, disappears whilst staying at an inn in Prussia. Piper describes Bathurst in the story as \"a rather stout gentleman, of past middle age.\" However, the real Bathurst was born in 1784, and thus 25 years old at the time of his disappearance.\nThis story posits that Bathurst slipped into a parallel universe. This event was referenced in the Paratime story \"Police Operation\", also written by Piper. The point of divergence from our history is the Battle of Quebec on December 31, 1775 in which Benedict Arnold is killed instead of merely wounded, leading to the victory of British General John Burgoyne over his American counterpart Horatio Gates at the Battles of Saratoga on September 19 and October 7, 1777. Consequently, the American Revolution and the French Revolution were both failures and there were no Napoleonic Wars.\nThe alternate Bathurst served as the lieutenant governor of the Crown Colony of Georgia. Napoleon Bonaparte is a colonel in the French Army who is considered a brilliant tactician. Charles Maurice de Talleyrand-PĂŠrigord has remained in ecclesiastical orders and risen to become a Catholic Cardinal as well as Louis XVI's Chief Minister. George Washington was killed in battle at Doylestown, Pennsylvania during the short-lived rebellion of the colonies in British North America. Moreover, Thomas Jefferson - the author of the American rebels' Declaration of Philadelphia - fled to Havana and eventually died in the Principality of Liechtenstein several years prior to 1809, while James Madison is in exile in Switzerland. The Bathurst from our timeline is judged to be either insane, or a spy, and imprisoned. He attempts to escape and is fatally shot. There was also a theory that he was his counterpart's half-brother. However, it is noted that there is no evidence to support this.\nBathurst's diplomatic documents are read by a high ranking British officer. He is amused by Talleyrand's role as Bonaparte's advisor and ĂŠminence grise, a role that he finds plausible. However, he is especially puzzled by references to a British general named \"Wellington.\" In the final line of the story, the British officer is revealed to be Sir Arthur Wellesley - known in our reality as the Duke of Wellington. He attained the title by way of his victories in the Napoleonic Wars, which never took place in this universe.\n\nQuestion: How did Benedict Arnold's death change history?\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Benedict Arnold's death at the Battle of Quebec in 1775 altered the course of the American Revolution, leading to British General John Burgoyne’s victory over Horatio Gates at Saratoga in 1777. This defeat caused the American Revolution to fail, preventing American independence and the subsequent French Revolution and Napoleonic Wars. As a result, Britain retained control of its North American colonies and European politics unfolded under a very different power structure.  \n\\boxed{\\text{Benedict Arnold's death led to a British victory at Saratoga, causing the American Revolution to fail and preventing the Napoleonic Wars.}}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 575,
        "output_tokens": 133,
        "total_tokens": 708
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": 0.00019805000000000002,
    "accuracy": 0.1642857142857143,
    "for_optimality": true
  },
