    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Benjamin Arnold’s death at the Battle of Quebec in 1775 (instead of being wounded) led to the British victory over the American forces at Saratoga in 1777. This altered outcome prevented the American Revolution’s success, resulting in no independent United States and the failure of the French Revolution as well. Consequently, the Napoleonic Wars never occurred, and Europe remained under monarchical control with figures like Napoleon still serving as a colonel and Talleyrand as a cardinal and chief minister.  \n\n\\boxed{\\text{Benedict Arnold's death led to a British victory at Saratoga, which prevented the success of the American Revolution and the subsequent Napoleonic Wars.}}",
