  {
    "global index": "NarrativeQA_7964",
    "prompt": "Please read the following context and answer the question based on its content.\n\nContext:  The story is set in a largely fictionalized version of Indianapolis, and much of it was inspired by the neighborhood of Woodruff Place.\nThe novel and trilogy trace the growth of the United States through the declining fortunes of three generations of the aristocratic Amberson family in an upper-scale Indianapolis neighborhood, between the end of the Civil War and the early part of the 20th century, a period of rapid industrialization and socio-economic change in America. The decline of the Ambersons is contrasted with the rising fortunes of industrial tycoons and other new-money families, who derived power not from family names but by \"doing things\". As George Amberson's friend (name unspecified) says, \"don't you think being things is 'rahthuh bettuh' than doing things?\"\nThe titular family is the most prosperous and powerful in town at the turn of the century. Young George Amberson Minafer, the patriarch's grandson, is spoiled terribly by his mother Isabel. Growing up arrogant, sure of his own worth and position, and totally oblivious to the lives of others, George falls in love with Lucy Morgan, a young though sensible debutante. But there is a long history between George's mother and Lucy's father, of which George is unaware. As the town grows into a city, industry thrives, the Ambersons' prestige and wealth wanes, and the Morgans, thanks to Lucy's prescient father, grow prosperous. When George sabotages his widowed mother's growing affections for Lucy's father, life as he knows it comes to an end.\n\nQuestion: What economic changes affect George's relationship with Lucy?\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The rising industrial economy and the shift from old aristocracy to new wealth impact George's relationship with Lucy. As the Amberson family's wealth and social status decline due to changing economic tides, the Morgan family prospers through industrial progress and foresight. George's inability to adapt to these changes—his pride rooted in inherited status rather than innovation—leads him to reject Lucy's father and ultimately lose Lucy, symbolizing the downfall of the old elite.\n\n\\boxed{{The decline of old wealth and rise of industrial new money undermine George's social standing, creating tension in his relationship with Lucy, whose family benefits from economic progress, ultimately leading to the relationship's collapse.}}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 456,
        "output_tokens": 188,
        "total_tokens": 644
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": 5.1176e-05,
    "accuracy": 0.23999999999999996,
    "for_optimality": true
  },
  {
    "global index": "NarrativeQA_7964",
    "prompt": "Please read the following context and answer the question based on its content.\n\nContext:  The story is set in a largely fictionalized version of Indianapolis, and much of it was inspired by the neighborhood of Woodruff Place.\nThe novel and trilogy trace the growth of the United States through the declining fortunes of three generations of the aristocratic Amberson family in an upper-scale Indianapolis neighborhood, between the end of the Civil War and the early part of the 20th century, a period of rapid industrialization and socio-economic change in America. The decline of the Ambersons is contrasted with the rising fortunes of industrial tycoons and other new-money families, who derived power not from family names but by \"doing things\". As George Amberson's friend (name unspecified) says, \"don't you think being things is 'rahthuh bettuh' than doing things?\"\nThe titular family is the most prosperous and powerful in town at the turn of the century. Young George Amberson Minafer, the patriarch's grandson, is spoiled terribly by his mother Isabel. Growing up arrogant, sure of his own worth and position, and totally oblivious to the lives of others, George falls in love with Lucy Morgan, a young though sensible debutante. But there is a long history between George's mother and Lucy's father, of which George is unaware. As the town grows into a city, industry thrives, the Ambersons' prestige and wealth wanes, and the Morgans, thanks to Lucy's prescient father, grow prosperous. When George sabotages his widowed mother's growing affections for Lucy's father, life as he knows it comes to an end.\n\nQuestion: What economic changes affect George's relationship with Lucy?\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The rise of industrialization and the emergence of new-money families like the Morgans shift economic power away from old aristocracy like the Ambersons, undermining George’s sense of entitlement. As Lucy’s father prospers through industrial enterprise, the Morgans’ rising status contrasts with the Ambersons’ decline, making George’s aristocratic pride increasingly out of step with the times. His sabotage of his mother’s relationship with Lucy’s father ultimately severs his connection to her, as economic change redefines social worth beyond inherited status.  \n$\\boxed{\\text{Industrialization and the rise of new-money families diminish the Ambersons' wealth and status, making George's aristocratic arrogance incompatible with Lucy's more pragmatic, rising family.}}$",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 385,
        "output_tokens": 151,
        "total_tokens": 536
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": 0.00020075000000000003,
    "accuracy": 0.2983783783783784,
    "for_optimality": true
