    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The excerpt describes the Sugar Act of 1764, which imposed duties on imported goods (especially sugar and molasses) in the American colonies to raise revenue for Britain and regulate colonial trade—reflecting mercantilist policies that prioritized protecting and expanding British commercial interests through tariffs. This aligns most closely with **protective tariffs established during the Industrial Revolution**, which aimed to shield domestic industries and generate government revenue by taxing imported goods. While the Sugar Act was specifically targeted at the colonies and predated the Industrial Revolution, its *mechanism and intent* (using tariffs to regulate trade and raise revenue for imperial purposes) are conceptually similar to later protective tariff policies.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
