    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4629",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\nI walk alongside the column, ask what's going on.\nA soldier says simply: \"They call up more every day.\n\"Some of us were sent north to the Yellow River at age fifteen,\nAnd now at forty we're heading off to the garrisons in the west.\nOn our first tour, the village headman had to tie our bandannas for us.\nWhen we came back, our hair was white, but still there's more unrest.\nThe frontier garrisons run with blood, enough to fill an ocean,\nBut the Martial Emperor's territorial ambitions have yet to crest.\nIn the hundred districts east of the mountains, throughout the land of Han,\nThere must be ten thousand villages that brambles now infest.\nEven if wives are strong enough to handle a hoe and plow,\nThe crops grow every which way, the fields are all a mess.\nIt's hardest for the Shanxi men, with their reputations as fighters:\nThey're rounded up like dogs or chickens, every male impressed.\n\"But sir, though it's good of you to ask,\nComplaining isn't part of the soldier's task.\nWe can only shake our heads. Take this winter:\nThe Shanxi troops were never sent home.\nThe District Officers are demanding the land tax,\nBut where will it come from? You can't get blood from a stone!\nI honestly think it's bad luck to bear a son now,\nIt's better to have a daughter: at least she can marry\nAnd live with the neighbors next door.\nBut a son will end up lying on some distant prairie.\"\nDu Fu, \"Ballad of the Army Carts,\" ca. 750 C.E.\nWhich of the following does the poem provide direct evidence for in its discussion of taxation in the third stanza?\n\nOptions: \nA. The Tang Dynasty's large, standing professional army with no need of conscription\nB. The Tang Dynasty's fair and equitable taxation system\nC. Relatively advanced Tang agricultural technologies\nD. The Tang Dynasty's strong bureaucracy at the local level\nE. The Tang Dynasty's economic prosperity due to high taxation\nF. The Tang Dynasty's lack of regular income due to unpaid taxes\nG. The Tang Dynasty's system of land distribution based on taxation\nH. The Tang Dynasty's heavy reliance on conscription to meet military needs\nI. The Tang Dynasty's system of forced labor in lieu of taxation\nJ. The relatively high level of urbanization in Tang China\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The poem directly describes District Officers demanding land tax despite villages being abandoned and farmland overrun by brambles, with the speaker asking, “Where will it come from? You can’t get blood from a stone!” — indicating revenue collection persists even when taxpayers cannot pay. This points to a persistent, active bureaucracy enforcing taxation amid economic collapse. The best answer is F: The Tang Dynasty's lack of regular income due to unpaid taxes.\n\n\\boxed{F}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 576,
        "output_tokens": 92,
        "total_tokens": 668
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": 0.00015304,
    "accuracy": 0.0,
    "for_optimality": true
  },
