    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4717",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\n\"Indeed, as both the fatwas of distinguished [scholars] who base their opinion on reason and tradition alike and the consensus of the Sunni community agree that the ancient obligation of extirpation, extermination, and expulsion of evil innovation must be the aim of our exalted aspiration, for \"Religious zeal is a victory for the Faith of God the Beneficent\"; then, in accordance with the words of the Prophet (Peace upon him!) \"Whosoever introduces evil innovation into our order must be expelled\" and \"Whosoever does aught against our order must be expelled,\" action has become necessary and exigent…\"\nLetter from Ottoman Sultan Selim I to Safavid Shah Ismail I, 1514\nThe Safavids drew the ire of Islamic empires such as the Ottoman and the Mughal Empires primarily because the Safavids\n\nOptions: \nA. had a more advanced military than the Ottoman and Mughal Empires\nB. were an expansionist empire geographically located between two other expansionists\nC. promoted polytheism, which went against the beliefs of the Ottoman and Mughal Empires\nD. were accused of blasphemy by the Ottoman and Mughal Empires\nE. Ismail proclaimed himself to be a Caliph\nF. refused to pay tributes to the Ottoman and Mughal Empires\nG. believed in a different sect of Islam\nH. were allies with the enemies of the Ottoman and Mughal Empires\nI. rejected the central tenets of Islam\nJ. enslaved Christians, and turned them into Safavid fighters\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The letter from Ottoman Sultan Selim I refers to the Safavids as promoters of \"evil innovation,\" indicating religious condemnation rooted in sectarian differences. The Safavids were Shi'a Muslims who actively promoted Shi'ism, especially under Shah Ismail I, which was viewed as heretical by the predominantly Sunni Ottoman and Mughal Empires. This religious divergence, not political or military actions alone, was the primary source of conflict.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 405,
        "output_tokens": 93,
        "total_tokens": 498
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
